From cef2@psu.edu Fri Sep 29 08:00:01 1995 CDT
Message-ID: <199509291259.IAA44366@r02n06.cac.psu.edu>
Date: Fri, 29 Sep 1995 08:59:49 -0400
From: Craig Farrell <cef2@psu.edu>
Subject: AXP NT blues

Hi.  I'm new to this list.  I have been trying to solve a network problem
with two DEC AXP2100 servers.  Below is a rather detailed discussion of what
is happening and what has been tried.  If anyone has any ideas please let me
know.  I have a call into DEC hardware support to get the IO modules
replaced but I don't know if that will help.

Craig Farrell - Penn State

I called DEC hardware support and they think all is well with the hardware
end.  The IO module is not the latest REV but they think that is ok.  Maybe
I should ask them to change it anyway.  I did try putting in a 3C509 3COM
ISA card and testing that.  It did the same but I didn't feel to good about
putting such a card in this machine.  I did pull the DC21040 driver during
the test.  Still got alot of Collisions.  I wish ALPHA supported 3C590 cards
as I wanted to put in a 100MB card.  Drivers and NT doesn't list it as a
supported adapter as yet.

I have two 2100 servers and they both do the same thing.  I have had both on
the network at the same time and I have had then on by themself.  Same
results no matter what server is online.  They are early release units from
about a year or so ago.  The SSN# is one number apart.  We asked for BETAII
release of NT so maybe some of the REV parts need to be looked at???

One server is running NT 3.51 and the other is running 3.5.  The 3.51 server
has the lastest firmware.  I did not upgrade the 3.5 server as yet since I
heard some rumors about 3.51.  So far so good.  Disk Admin seeems to have
some problems with 3.51.  I think if you have 2 or less partitions on a disk
you are ok.  I will be upgrading the 3.5 soon I quess.  3.5 has been rock solid.

I called DEC NT software support.  I FTP what they said was the lastest
drivers.  Might even be newer then the ones on the WEB page you mentioned.
DEC in 3.51 changed the name of the driver from DC21040.SYS to DC21X4.SYS.
I have version 2.41 of the DC21X4.SYS driver.  They also gave me a driver
with the same name for the 3.5 system.  This new drivers seems to be for a
DE435 card but I think it has the same controller as the integrated IO
module in the 2100.  No luck.

I have tried using AUI and UTP.  Both give same results.  AUI cable is very
short and I had that plugged into a DELNI.  The servers did not like being
in a DELNI if the global port was closed.  I since have placed the servers
on a David Systems 10baseT hub.  The DC21X4.SYS setup in NT has three
choices for 10baseT.  10baseT, Full duplex or no_link_test.  I have tried
all but full duplex.  I tried no_link_test and turned off this on the hub.
No luck.  What setting should I use?  I figured 10baseT should work.  All
these tests have been done with the HUB off our campus LAN.  Meaning the AUI
connector on the HUB is not connected to anything.  I have tried just two
servers on the hub.  I tried one server and one windows for workgroups
machine.  These combinations still have problems.  Two WFW machines on the
HUB with the servers off.  No problem at all except I have no servers!!!

DEC NT support kind of gave up on me.  They say some collisions are normal.
I get a bunch.  Something here is wrong and I seem to have tried all I know.
I don't know anyone that has such systems so I can't compare.  You say you
are not.  First contact I have had with another user.  I could not beleive
DEC would make a product with an ethernet connector that would not work on a
LAN correctly.  Seems like the servers are just dumping on the net and don't
seem to care to much about it.  My end users are suffering because of this
and I'm getting some heat over it.  Often PC are dropping LAN connections or
are acting strange as they can't seem to deal with the massive amounts of
collisions.

Sorry for being long winded but I wanted to get you all the details in case
you have a suggestion.  I'm thinking hardware more and more but that 3COM
test I did makes me think not.  What is really strange is that both servers
have the same problems.  They did follow each other off the assembly line.
DEC did have me try some console tests that they said proved the cards we
ok.  Any ideas???????????

Craig Farrell - Coord. Computer & Information Services

Penn State Altoona Campus
3000 Ivyside Park
Altoona PA 16601

PHONE (814) 949-5295
FAX   (814) 949-5547

